etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4187439\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71569181\nTEXT: US President Donald Trump claims to have reached a deal with Iran, but questions remain about the agreement\'s details. The memorandum of understanding is expected to be finalized this weekend, but Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu\'s office has stated it will not be part of the deal. Trump believes Ayatollah Mojtaba Khamenei, Iran\'s supreme leader, has signed off on the agreement. However, a spokesperson for Iran\'s Foreign Ministry says the text of the deal has not been finalized yet. The agreement would immediately reopen the Strait of Hormuz, a crucial oil shipping lane, and Iran has committed not to pursue a nuclear weapon.\n\nID: 4187440\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71573017\nTEXT: The US military\'s secret mission to capture Iran\'s uranium has been paused, with President Trump citing the high risk of casualties. According to sources familiar with the plans, Iran has been plotting an economic "nuclear option" if negotiations fail, including closing the Bab-al-Mandab strait and getting its proxy force in Yemen to block it. Iran\'s version of the deal is different, with no commitment to ceding management of the Strait of Hormuz and a halt to funding terrorist groups. The US has expressed skepticism that it can locate and verify all the uranium, with nuclear experts warning of the challenges in safely removing it under hostile conditions.\n\nID: 4187216\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: thedailystar.net\nSLUG: mediator-pakistan-says-iran-us-peace-deal-text-agreed-4197286\nTEXT: Pakistan\'s mediator, Sharif, confirmed a final agreed-upon text for the peace deal between Iran and the US has been reached. Sharif posted on X, tagging both US President Donald Trump and Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi. Pakistan has been mediating between the two sides for months, with Sharif stating "Peace has never been as close as it is now." However, Araghchi insisted that a deal with the US to end the war in the Middle East had never been closer, after Trump accused Tehran of negotiating in bad faith.\n\nID: 4187403\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: thenationalnews.com\nSLUG: live-trump-iran-us-deal-text-pakistan-shebaz-sharif\nTEXT: The US and Iran have reached a final, agreed upon text for their deal. Pakistani Prime Minister Shebaz Sharif stated that the agreement is a significant step towards peace in the region. The deal, which has been under negotiation for several days, aims to reduce tensions between the two countries. The US claims that an imminent deal is likely, which has led to a drop in oil prices below $90. The US and Iran have been engaged in diplomatic efforts, with Egyptian officials urging both countries to seize the opportunity for a deal.\n\nID: 4187093\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: scmp.com\nSLUG: us-and-iran-inch-closer-signing-deal-reopen-strait-hormuz\nTEXT: The US and Iran are close to signing a deal to reopen the Strait of Hormuz, a critical waterway in the Middle East. The agreement, likely to be signed during next week\'s G7 summit in Evian, France, would also extend their truce by around two months. A senior Iranian official indicated that a deal is likely, while a G7 official and an outside diplomat confirmed the talks. The pact aims to address tensions between the two nations, which have been at odds over Iran\'s nuclear program and US sanctions.\n\nID: 4187587\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: thestar.com\nSLUG: article-8f24575e-8a08-5864-a13a-17878768bfc3-html\nTEXT: US President Donald Trump claims to be close to a deal with Iran ahead of a whirlwind weekend, but the situation is tense due to disagreements over tariffs and Ukraine. The Iran war is also a point of contention, with the US and Iran trading barbs. A key surveillance program, Section 702, is set to expire on Friday due to failed bipartisan efforts to extend it. However, a court order from March has authorized the program\'s continued use for another year, potentially mitigating an immediate drop-off.\n\nID: 4187214\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: thedailystar.net\nSLUG: iran-says-deal-us-closer-ever-trump-lashes-out-4197291\nTEXT: US President Donald Trump has angrily dismissed Iran\'s account of a draft deal, claiming it bears no relation to the terms agreed upon. In contrast, Iran\'s foreign minister Abbas Araghchi appeared to play down the row, stating that the Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding has never been closer. The draft deal, published by Iran\'s Mehr news agency, would end the war on all fronts, including Lebanon, and include the release of $24 billion in Iran\'s frozen assets.\n\nID: 4186873\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: manilatimes.net\nSLUG: 2364541\nTEXT: Iran has made no commitment to cede control of the Hormuz Strait, according to a draft US deal. The Islamic Republic News Agency stated that Tehran\'s official news agency referred to "the broad outlines of the current text" being finalized. US President Donald Trump claimed a deal could be signed in the coming days, but Iran\'s foreign ministry spokesman Esmaeil Baqaei said the country has "not reached a final conclusion" on an agreement.\n\nID: 4187452\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: gulftoday.ae\nSLUG: mediator-pakistan-pm-says-iran-us-peace-deal-text-agreed\nTEXT: Pakistan\'s Prime Minister Shahbaz has stated that peace is closer than ever, amid intense mediation efforts. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi has insisted that a deal with the US to end the war in the Middle East is closer than ever, despite US President Donald Trump accusing Tehran of negotiating in bad faith. Pakistan\'s Sharif added that there is an incessant misinformation campaign being waged to sabotage the peace deal.\n\nID: 4187197\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: apnews.com\nSLUG: iran-us-ceasefire-hezbollah-israel-12-june-2026-7085e386e1c40ee6cfe634210970143f\nTEXT: Pakistan\'s Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif announced that the US and Iran have reached a "final, agreed upon text" for an agreement aimed at ending their war in the Middle East. This breakthrough comes after Iran exchanged fire with the US and Israel over three days, threatening to return the region to full-scale war. The fragile ceasefire, which has been in place since April 7, was launched by the US and Israel on February 28.\n\nID: 4187268\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: cbs8.com\nSLUG: 509-c2438a21-9fb5-4587-a836-13286b4534df\nTEXT: President Trump claims a possible Iran deal has been reached to end the war, citing a meeting with Iranian officials. According to sources, Trump stated that the deal could be finalized within weeks and would bring an end to the conflict. The proposed agreement includes concessions from Iran, such as limiting its nuclear program and reducing its military presence in the region.\n\nID: 4187459\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: gulftoday.ae\nSLUG: iran-agrees-to-dismantle-nuclear-programme-under-deal-claims-white-house\nTEXT: Iran has agreed to dismantle its nuclear program and destroy nuclear material under a deal with the US, according to a White House official. The agreement includes Iran opening the Strait of Hormuz and not receiving frozen funds until it honors its commitments. The deal is performance-based, with no specific timeline or details provided.\n\nID: 4187095\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: scmp.com\nSLUG: iran-says-peace-deal-us-never-been-closer-pakistan-says-final-text-agreed\nTEXT: Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi stated that the US and Iran have "never been closer" to a peace deal, following talks in Islamabad. Pakistan claims a final text has been agreed upon, but US President Trump slammed reports about the agreement citing leaked terms that appear to favor Tehran.\n\n\n',)
