etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4187591\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: geo.tv\nSLUG: 668450-fy27-budget-govt-proposes-major-tax-relief-for-property-buyers-sellers\nTEXT: Pakistan\'s Finance Minister Muhammad Aurangzeb presented a budget with a total outlay of Rs18.771 trillion, aiming to encourage investment in the property sector through tax relief measures. The proposed tax relief includes reducing withholding tax on property purchases from 2.5% to 1.5% and cutting tax on property sales from 5.5% to 2.75%. The digital economy is also expected to benefit, with IT and IT-enabled services exports reaching $3.8 billion by the end of the current fiscal year. The concessional Final Tax Regime (FTR) rate for IT companies, set to expire on June 30, 2026, is proposed to be extended for another three years until June 30, 2029.\n\nID: 4187617\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: geo.tv\nSLUG: 668440-pakistan-income-tax-calculator-2026-27-check-your-salary-tax-cut\nTEXT: Finance Minister Muhammad Aurangzeb presented the Federal Budget 2026-27, outlining new income tax measures for the salaried class. The government proposed income tax relief in four slabs for salaried individuals, with the applicable marginal tax rate reduced from 23% to 20% for those earning between Rs2.2 million and Rs3.2 million annually, and from 30% to 25% for those earning between Rs3.2 million and Rs4.1 million annually. The proposed tax rates are as follows: Rs116,000 plus 20% of the amount exceeding Rs2.2 million for the first slab, and Rs316,000 plus 25% of the amount exceeding Rs3.2 million for the second slab.\n\nID: 4187616\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: geo.tv\nSLUG: 668428-govt-proposes-7-salary-pension-hike-in-fy2026-27-budget\nTEXT: The Pakistani government has proposed a 7% increase in salaries for public sector employees and a 7% increase in pensions for retired government employees in the FY2026-27 budget. Finance Minister Muhammad Aurangzeb unveiled the proposal during his National Assembly budget speech, citing Pakistan\'s growing reputation as a respected nation. The government also suggested a 10% increase in the minimum wage and proposed income tax relief for salaried individuals, with four new tax slabs introduced. Additionally, the government plans to abolish the surcharge on the salaried class and reduce income tax rates for taxpayers.\n\nID: 4187638\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: geo.tv\nSLUG: 668479-govt-to-abolish-super-tax-on-exporters-completely-says-finmin-aurangzeb\nTEXT: Pakistan\'s Finance Minister Aurangzeb announced plans to abolish the super tax on exporters completely in the FY2026-27 budget. The finance minister emphasized the need for a buffer in the budget due to the impact of energy infrastructure on the economy. The proposed budget allocates Rs8.054 trillion for mark-up payments, Rs3 trillion for defence, and Rs1 trillion for the federal development programme. Aurangzeb also proposed income tax relief in four slabs for salaried individuals and the abolition of the surcharge on the salaried class.\n\nID: 4187417\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: thestar.com\nSLUG: article-e9028835-ec2d-55c2-8e55-97791949414f-html\nTEXT: Pakistan\'s Finance Minister, Muhammad Aurangzeb, presented the country\'s new budget to lawmakers on Friday, which hikes defense spending by 18% while reducing funding for development programs and offering modest tax reliefs. The proposal aims to achieve 4% economic growth and an inflation of 8.2% for the coming year, despite concerns over price pressures. The budget closely follows conditions under Pakistan\'s $7 billion program with the International Monetary Fund, which has pushed for higher revenues and broader taxation.\n\nID: 4186985\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: apnews.com\nSLUG: pakkistan-budget-parliament-spending-7489d3dcf439da0ad106c3a85884e480\nTEXT: Pakistan\'s parliament will vote on a budget proposal later this month, aiming for 4% economic growth and an inflation rate of 8.2%. The budget closely follows conditions under Pakistan\'s $7 billion program with the International Monetary Fund, which has pushed for higher revenues and broader taxation.\n\n\n',)
