etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4187503\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: globalnews.ca\nSLUG: david-hockney-british-artist-dies-at-88\nTEXT: David Hockney, a renowned British artist, has passed away at the age of 88. In his later years, Hockney turned to drawing on iPads, which became his preferred medium. His 2017 exhibition at Tate Britain in London featured landscape paintings that combined bold colors with minute attention to texture, drawing inspiration from Yorkshire\'s fields and forests. His work was showcased at the Pompidou Center in Paris and the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York. Hockney also designed a stained-glass window for Westminster Abbey to celebrate Queen Elizabeth II\'s long reign. During the 2020 coronavirus lockdown, he produced iPad drawings of springtime for his friends, with a message that "they can\'t cancel the spring" emblazoned across the Fondation Louis Vuitton in Paris.\n\nID: 4187067\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: channelnewsasia.com\nSLUG: david-hockney-dies-age-88-british-artist-6179996\nTEXT: David Hockney, a renowned British artist, has passed away at the age of 88. His early work showcased a bold stylistic range, earning him recognition as a master draughtman and rising star in British art. Hockney\'s iconic works captured California\'s carefree spirit and Yorkshire\'s bucolic landscapes, with his portraits being particularly beloved. He set a new auction record for a living artist in 2018 with his swimming pool picture, "Portrait of an Artist (Pool with Two Figures)," selling for $90.3 million in New York. Hockney\'s work continues to influence culture, far beyond the art world, with his legacy being celebrated by institutions such as Tate Britain and the National Portrait Gallery.\n\nID: 4186802\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: manilatimes.net\nSLUG: 2364543\nTEXT: David Hockney, a renowned British artist known for his vibrant landscapes and pool scenes, has passed away at 88. His iconic works were showcased in a 2017 exhibition at Tate Britain, attracting half a million visitors. Hockney designed a stained-glass window for Westminster Abbey in 2018, celebrating Queen Elizabeth II\'s long reign. During the 2020 coronavirus lockdown, he created joyful iPad drawings of springtime for his friends, with a message "Do remember they can\'t cancel the spring" emblazoned across the Fondation Louis Vuitton in Paris. Curator Norman Rosenthal praised Hockney as "the Picasso of our times," highlighting his impact on the art world.\n\nID: 4187590\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: koat.com\nSLUG: 71570800\nTEXT: David Hockney, the renowned artist, has passed away at 88. In a 1995 interview, Hockney stated that he considered himself "very much an artist in the English tradition," despite his admiration for American pop art. His move to California in 1964 was influenced by earlier English artists seeking the Italian light, a precedent he noted. Hockney\'s works, such as "We Two Boys Together Clinging" and "Two Men in a Shower," celebrated gay relationships, a theme he explored with tender scrutiny. His early drawings were bought by the Museum of Modern Art in New York, and he once said that selling his first pictures made him feel "rich."\n\nID: 4187092\nCREATED: 2026-06-12\nDOMAIN: scmp.com\nSLUG: britains-david-hockney-giant-contemporary-art-dies-aged-88\nTEXT: David Hockney, a renowned contemporary artist, has died at 88. He continued painting and exhibiting until his death, leaving behind a seven-decade career marked by his multimedia approach to image making. Hockney\'s work captured the world in brilliant colour, earning him a place as one of the most influential figures in contemporary art. His publicist, Erica Bolton, described him as "one of the most important figures in contemporary art in both the 20th and 21st centuries".\n\n\n',)
